.TH HD-REPORT 8 "October 2026" "hd-report" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-report \- send the state of the system to the hyprdebian dashboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-report
.RB [ \-\-print ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-report
collects the state of the system and POSTs it as a JSON report to the
hyprdebian dashboard, see
.BR hd-dashboard (7).
The system only makes outgoing connections.
.PP
.B hd-report.timer
runs it through
.B hd-report.service
2 minutes after the boot and then every 15 minutes, with up to a minute of
random delay.
The package enables the timer on its first installation; after that, it stays
as the admin left it.
When the dashboard cannot be reached or refuses the report, the service fails
and the error is in its journal:
.PP
.RS
journalctl \-u hd-report.service
.RE
.PP
Run it as root: the token is only readable by root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ,\  \-\-help
Show a short help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-print
Only print the report, without sending it.
.SH SETTINGS
The settings are the key
.B hd-report
of
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ,
see
.BR hd-config (7).
A setting that is missing has its default value, from
.IR /usr/share/hd-report/defaults.json .
.TP
.B url
The URL of the dashboard, with
.B http://
or
.BR https:// ;
the report goes to
.IR <url>/report .
The default is
.BR http://dash.rsq\-online.ddns.net/ .
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS
sudo hd-config-update hd-report '{"url": "http://dash.example/"}'
.RE
.SH TOKEN
The token of the system is in
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/hd-report/token ,
mode 0600.
The installation generates it when there is none, and never replaces it:
the dashboard accepts the first token it sees for a host name, and only that
token afterwards.
A purge of the package keeps it too.
When the token is lost, as on a reinstalled system, forget the host on the
dashboard with
.BR "hd-dashboard forget" ,
and the next report registers the new token.
.SH THE REPORT
The host name, the FQDN, the version of HyprDebian from
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/version.json ,
the suite of the HyprDebian packages, the Debian
version, the running and the newest installed kernel, the boot time, the
state of Secure Boot as
.BR hd-secboot-check (8)
finds it, the load, the CPUs, the memory, the ZFS pools with their last
completed scrub, the mdadm arrays, the
state of systemd and the failed units, the upgradable packages from the
package lists apt has now, the last upgrade, a pending
reboot, the newest
.B hd-up
snapshot of
.IR rpool/hyprdebian ,
and the enabled timers in
.I /etc/systemd/system
and the
.B hd-*
timers of the packages, with the result of their last run.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the report was sent or printed, 1 on an error, 2 on wrong arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/config.json
The settings, in the key
.BR hd-report .
.TP
.I /usr/share/hd-report/defaults.json
The default settings.
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/hd-report/token
The token of the system.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/hd-report.timer
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/hd-report.service
Run hd-report every 15 minutes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-dashboard (7),
.BR hd-config-update (1),
.BR hd-secboot-check (8),
.BR hd-up (1)
